    "q-210758": {
      "dom_id": "step-q210758",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}6{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-6t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
